Flaky: update tests race on OPENPLANET_LSP_PACKAGE_MANAGER env var

Observed 2026-08-29 during B031's full-suite run; unrelated to that work.

## Symptom
`cargo test --locked` intermittently fails:

    ---- update::tests::detect_npm_local_layout stdout ----
    panicked at src/update.rs:1722:22:
    expected NodeLocal npm, got NodeLocal { pm: Pnpm, package_root: "/work/my-plugin" }

Passes in isolation (`cargo test --lib update::tests::detect_npm_local_layout`)
and on a re-run of the full suite, so CI will see it as a random red.

## Cause
`package_manager_env_override` (src/update.rs:1898) does

    std::env::set_var("OPENPLANET_LSP_PACKAGE_MANAGER", "pnpm");
    ...
    std::env::remove_var("OPENPLANET_LSP_PACKAGE_MANAGER");   // :1908

Env vars are process-global and cargo runs `--lib` tests as parallel THREADS
in one process, so any test whose detection consults that variable can read
it during the ~10 lines it is set. `detect_npm_local_layout` is one such
test; `detect_npm_local_layout` expects the lockfile-derived `npm` and gets
the env override's `pnpm`.

`status_roundtrip_in_temp_config_dir` (:1921) does the same with
`OPENPLANET_LSP_CONFIG_DIR`, so it is the same hazard, currently unobserved.

## Fix options
1. A test-local mutex around every test that sets or reads those vars
   (a `static ENV_LOCK: Mutex<()>` in the test module). Smallest change,
   keeps the env-override coverage.
2. Thread the override through a parameter instead of reading the env inside
   the detection fn, so tests never touch process state. Cleaner seam, more
   churn.
3. `#[serial]` via a dev-dependency. Rejected: a new dep for two tests.

Recommend (1) now, (2) if the env-read seam grows.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red is inherently racy, so make it deterministic: a test that sets the var,
spawns a thread calling the detection fn on a lockfile fixture, and asserts
the lockfile answer wins per-call — fails today, passes once the seam or the
lock is in place. Variants: the config-dir var (same hazard), and a run of
the whole `update::tests` module under `--test-threads=8` repeated to show
the flake is gone.